LSEEK(2)                       P32W Unix                        LSEEK(2)

NAME
       lseek - move the read/write offset of a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       off_t lseek(int fd, off_t offset, int whence);

DESCRIPTION
       lseek sets the file offset of the open file fd, where the
       next read(2) or write(2) starts:

       whence 0   to offset
       whence 1   to the current offset plus offset
       whence 2   to the size of the file plus offset

       (<unistd.h> does not name these; V7 programs use the
       numbers, POSIX ones SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, SEEK_END.) The offset
       may go past the end of the file; a later write there leaves
       a hole that reads as zeros. The offset is shared by every
       descriptor that dup(2) or fork(2) made from the same open.

       lseek(fd, 0, 1) tells where the offset is.

RETURN VALUE
       The new offset; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   fd is not an open file descriptor.
       ESPIPE  fd is a pipe, or a character device other than
               /dev/rdisk (a terminal, /dev/null, /dev/port).
       EINVAL  whence is not 0, 1 or 2, or the new offset would be
               negative.

SEE ALSO
       read(2), write(2), open(2), stdio(3)

BUGS
       Offsets are 32-bit signed numbers: files and devices beyond
       2 GiB cannot be reached. SEEK_SET and friends are not
       defined. For a block device whence 2 uses the size of the
       special file, which is 0.
